  "Number": 11,
  "Question": "Suppose that \\((u_n)\\) is a sequence of real numbers satisfying \\(u_{n+2}=2u_{n+1}+u_n,\\) and that \\(u_3=9\\) and \\(u_6=128.\\) What is \\(u_5?\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(40\\)",
    "B": "\\(53\\)",
    "C": "\\(68\\)",
    "D": "\\(88\\)",
    "E": "\\(104\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "Use the unknown term \\(u_4\\) to connect the two given terms. Let \\(u_4=t\\). Applying the recurrence with \\(n=3\\) gives\n\\[\nu_5=2u_4+u_3=2t+9.\n\\]\nApplying it next with \\(n=4\\) gives\n\\[\nu_6=2u_5+u_4\n=2(2t+9)+t\n=5t+18.\n\\]\nSince \\(u_6=128\\), we have\n\\[\n5t+18=128,\n\\qquad t=\\frac{110}{5}=22.\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\nu_5=2(22)+9=\\boxed{53},\n\\]\nchoice \\(\\boxed{\\text{B}}\\).",
